<dialog id="instructions-modal" class="admin-modal admin-modal-wide" aria-label="Instructions">
  <div class="admin-modal-card game-instructions-card">
    <div class="cluster-browse-toolbar">
      <h2>Instructions</h2>
      <button type="button" class="btn-secondary" data-instructions-close>Close</button>
    </div>
    <div class="game-instructions-body">
      <h2 id="playerWelcome" style="display: none;"></h2>
      <span id="changeName" style="font-size: 0.8em;">(<a href="#" onClick="changeName(); return false;">Change name</a>)</span>
      <h3>Can you beat the machine learning system? or other players?</h3>
      <p>If the video does not load, follow the written steps on this page. The game works without it.</p>
      <div class="game-instructions-video"><iframe src="https://player.vimeo.com/video/888716313?h=839683ea20&amp;badge=0&amp;autopause=0&amp;player_id=0&amp;app_id=58479" frameborder="0" allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" title="Machine Learning Game Video One"></iframe></div>
      <p>Your challenge is to create a decision tree that you think can successfully classify properties as either being in New York or San Francisco. Your decision must be based upon a limited number of characteristics.</p>
      <h4>Training your decision tree</h4>
      <p>On the <a href="#play" class="play-mode-link" data-play-mode="training">Play</a> screen, build your tree with the training cards. Hover the stack to browse cards, drag them into leaf boxes, or use <b>Run model</b> to sort them.</p>
      <p>On each leaf, choose a city prediction, hover for <b>Add branch</b>, or <b>Browse cluster</b> once cards are in that box.</p>
      <p>Short guided demos are in the <b>Tutorials</b> panel: <b>Getting started</b>, <b>Exploring the training data</b>, and <b>Testing and evaluating your tree</b>. The first tour starts automatically the first time you visit training; you can skip or replay any of them.</p>
      <h4>Testing and evaluating</h4>
      <p>After a training run, the card box shows how many cards matched each leaf prediction. Use <b>Run a test?</b> for a purple holdout set, or <b>Evaluate</b> for your final scored runs. A class uses the session evaluation set; a self-paced game draws cards at random.</p>
      <p>Good luck!</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</dialog>
